{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 58, :comments ["Rashi writes: <b>\"These are the families of Levi\"</b> — missing here are the family of the Shimeites, the Uzzielites, and some of the descendants of Yitshar, namely Nepheg and Zikri. And I wonder: why would the verse enumerate families in Levi for the three patriarchs and then go back and enumerate families for the sons, since Merari had no other sons but Mahli and Mushi — so what is \"the family of the Merarites\" beyond them? Rather, this was for honor: the three sons of Levi were all great in Israel and their memory was for a blessing. All the descendants of Gershon were called \"the family of the Gershonites\" in his honor, and likewise all the sons of Merari were called \"the family of the Merarites\" in his honor. Afterward they established for themselves fathers' houses and were divided into families like the rest of the tribes, and they were designated specifically as the family of the Mahlites and the family of the Mushites. So too with Kehath: all his seed was called in general \"the family of the Kehathites,\" and they again divided into families according to the sons: the family of the Hebronites and the family of the Korhites. And he did not mention now \"the family of the Amramites\" because it was split into priests and Levites, and he mentioned their names for honor, and he did not mention the priesthood of Aaron out of honor for Moses. So too you will see in the first census, where it says: \"And these were the sons of Levi by their names: Gershon and Kehath and Merari\" (Numbers 3:17) — and this was for their distinction, for among the rest of the tribes he did not do so. And the reason for <b>\"the family of the Korhites\"</b> is that at first it was called \"the family of the Yitsharites\" (ibid. v. 27), for at the time of that census there were sons also for Nepheg and Zikri, but now there were only those of Korah and they were called by his name. In my opinion it was not proper that the name of the Yitsharites be uprooted from his seed, even though some of his sons died; rather, they did this after the affair of Korah for the honor of his sons, to make their name and memory known in Israel for all generations, for thus the verse takes pains to say: \"And the sons of Korah did not die\" (Numbers 26:11). And since Nepheg and Zikri perished and all the remaining seed of Yitshar belonged to Korah, they called them \"the family of the Korhites\" to make known that his sons were righteous and better than he. And the midrash of Rabbi Moshe ha-Darshan on \"You are the fewest of all the peoples\" — five, is not correct, for he counts for the sons of Levi eight, when they are only five, since the three belong to their patriarchs as we have said."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:16.096862159Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}